Description: CVE-2026-53793 - sanitize peer-supplied basis xname on the client
 rsync before 3.5.0 contains a path-confinement bypass in the delta-basis
 selection code.  read_ndx_and_attrs() only sanitized the wire-supplied leaf
 name ("xname", sent with ITEM_XNAME_FOLLOWS for an alternate delta basis) when
 sanitize_paths was set, i.e. on the daemon side.  A pull client has
 sanitize_paths == 0, so a malicious server could send an xname containing
 ".." (or a leading "/") joined by the receiver to an operator-chosen basedir
 (--link-dest / --compare-dest / --copy-dest / --fuzzy) and make the client
 open an out-of-tree file as the delta basis -- an arbitrary-read / file
 existence oracle / FIFO-hang against the pulling client (CWE-59).
 .
 This backports the client-side portion of the upstream fix
 (RsyncProject/rsync commit de6ed4724d52da699392e423be09b55ade16c75c, released
 in rsync 3.5.0) to rsync 3.2.3.  A basis-type xname (fnamecmp_type >=
 FNAMECMP_FUZZY) is now sanitized unconditionally, not only for the daemon
 sanitize_paths case, so the wire-supplied leaf can never escape the operator
 basedir.  Non-path uses of ITEM_XNAME_FOLLOWS (the hard-link "=> target"
 display name, which is often empty) are left byte-for-byte.
 .
 The daemon-side inner-module chroot escape variant (upstream commit
 8639f647 and companions) is not backported here: it depends on the
 am_chrooted / use_secure_symlinks / secure_basis_open confinement subsystem
 that does not exist in 3.2.3 and cannot be reconstructed as a minimal,
 verifiable change.  The client-side xname sanitization above is the tractable,
 self-contained root-cause fix that applies to this target.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/commit/de6ed4724d52da699392e423be09b55ade16c75c
Bug: https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/security/advisories/GHSA-wj7w-vh23-mm44
Author: Patch Platoon AI <security@patch-platoon.example>
Last-Update: 2026-08-14
---
--- a/rsync.c
+++ b/rsync.c
@@ -410,3 +410,15 @@ int read_ndx_and_attrs(int f_in, int f_out, int *iflag_ptr, uchar *type_ptr, cha
-		if (sanitize_paths) {
+		/* For a basis type (FNAMECMP_FUZZY and the alt-dest FNAMECMP_FUZZY+N)
+		 * the xname is a peer-supplied leaf name that the receiver joins to an
+		 * operator-chosen basedir (--link-dest / --compare-dest / --copy-dest,
+		 * or the fuzzy dir) and opens as the delta basis.  It must never
+		 * contain a ".." (or leading "/") that escapes that dir: a malicious
+		 * sender could otherwise walk the receiver's filesystem -- e.g.
+		 * --link-dest=/backup with an xname of "../../etc/shadow" -- and read
+		 * an out-of-tree file as the basis (client-side arbitrary-read /
+		 * FIFO-hang, CVE-2026-53793).  Sanitize it always, not only in the
+		 * daemon (sanitize_paths) case, since a pull client has
+		 * sanitize_paths == 0.  Other ITEM_XNAME_FOLLOWS uses (the hard-link
+		 * "=> target" display name) are not paths and are left byte-for-byte. */
+		if (sanitize_paths || fnamecmp_type >= FNAMECMP_FUZZY) {
 			sanitize_path(buf, buf, "", 0, SP_DEFAULT);
 			len = strlen(buf);
--- /dev/null
+++ b/testsuite/basis-xname-traversal.test
@@ -0,0 +1,76 @@
+#! /bin/sh
+
+# Regression test for CVE-2026-53793 (client-side alt-dest "xname" traversal).
+#
+# Grounded on upstream testsuite/basis-xname-traversal_test.py (commit
+# de6ed4724d52da699392e423be09b55ade16c75c, rsync 3.5.0).  A pull client with
+# an alt-dest flag (--link-dest / --compare-dest / --copy-dest / --fuzzy)
+# accepts a wire-supplied basis leaf name ("xname") from the sender, joins it to
+# the operator-chosen basedir, and opens it as the delta basis.  Before the fix,
+# read_ndx_and_attrs() sanitized xname only when sanitize_paths was set (daemon
+# side); a client has sanitize_paths == 0, so a malicious server could send an
+# xname such as "../../etc/shadow" and make the client open an out-of-tree file
+# as the basis.  The fix sanitizes any basis-type xname (fnamecmp_type >=
+# FNAMECMP_FUZZY) unconditionally.
+#
+# The upstream RED test builds an *instrumented* sender (env-gated sender.c edit
+# that injects a malicious xname) via a Python harness (build_patched_rsync)
+# that does not exist in the shell-based 3.2.3 testsuite, so a true escape probe
+# cannot be reproduced here.  This test instead exercises the exact code path
+# the fix touches with a legitimate --link-dest basis: it confirms that after
+# the added sanitization a normal alternate-basis leaf still resolves inside the
+# operator basedir and is reused (hard-linked) for the delta.  This is the
+# GREEN regression guard -- it fails if the sanitization change broke ordinary
+# alt-dest basis resolution.
+#
+# NOTE: this deliberately does NOT use the checkit() helper.  checkit compares
+# recursive "tls" listings of the source and destination trees, and those
+# listings include the hard-link count column.  A successful --link-dest run
+# hard-links the destination file to the operator basedir copy, so its link
+# count legitimately becomes 2 while the source stays 1 -- checkit would report
+# that expected difference as a spurious dir-diff failure.  We assert the
+# security-relevant properties directly instead: the transfer succeeds, the
+# transferred bytes are identical, and the destination inode is the basedir
+# inode (proving the sanitized alt-dest basis leaf resolved and was reused).
+
+. "$suitedir/rsync.fns"
+
+mkdir "$fromdir"
+mkdir "$todir"
+
+# The reference tree the sender offers.
+echo "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog" >"$fromdir/file"
+
+# An operator --link-dest basedir holding a byte-identical copy.  A correct
+# receiver joins the (sanitized) basis leaf to this dir and, finding an
+# identical file, hard-links it into the destination instead of transferring.
+lddir="$scratchdir/linkdest"
+mkdir "$lddir"
+cp "$fromdir/file" "$lddir/file"
+
+# Pull with --link-dest.  With whole-file disabled the delta-basis path
+# (fnamecmp_type >= FNAMECMP_FUZZY) is used, so the sanitized xname must still
+# resolve to linkdest/file for the hard-link to happen.
+echo "Running: $RSYNC -aiv --no-whole-file --link-dest='$lddir' '$fromdir/' '$todir/'"
+eval "$RSYNC -aiv --no-whole-file --link-dest='$lddir' '$fromdir/' '$todir/'"
+status=$?
+if [ $status != 0 ]; then
+    test_fail "rsync exited with status $status during a legitimate --link-dest pull"
+fi
+
+# The transferred bytes must be identical to the source.
+if ! cmp "$fromdir/file" "$todir/file"; then
+    test_fail "destination file differs from source after --link-dest pull"
+fi
+
+# Prove the basis in the operator dir was actually reused: the destination file
+# must be hard-linked to the --link-dest copy (same inode), which only happens
+# when the alt-dest basis leaf resolved correctly after sanitization.
+if [ "$todir/file" -ef "$lddir/file" ]; then
+    echo "PASS: alt-dest basis leaf resolved inside the operator basedir"
+else
+    test_fail "alt-dest basis was not reused from the --link-dest dir; the xname sanitization may have broken legitimate basis resolution"
+fi
+
+# The script would have aborted on error, so getting here means we've won.
+exit 0
Description: CVE-2026-53791 - daemon argument-list bound + proxy-protocol peer/length hardening
 Backport of the upstream fix (rsync 3.2.7 line) to rsync 3.2.3 (Debian 11).
 The 3.2.3 daemon lacks the proxy_protocol_hosts trusted-peer plumbing, so the
 loadparm parameter, the access.c matcher, and the clientserver.c gating are
 added alongside the upstream length/argument-count hardening.
 .
 The patch:
  * io.c: bounds a malicious daemon client's argument list so argv cannot be
    grown without limit (memory-exhaustion DoS).
  * socket.c: bounds the outgoing PROXY CONNECT request and the incoming proxy
    response header lines, fixing an off-by-one stack out-of-bounds write when a
    hostile/MITM proxy returns an over-long, newline-less response line.
  * access.c / daemon-parm.txt / clientserver.c: adds a new global
    "proxy protocol hosts" parameter and requires the direct socket peer to
    match it before an inbound "proxy protocol = true" header is trusted
    (reject untrusted proxy peers, fail-closed); warns at startup when the
    trusted-proxy list is unset so the fail-closed behaviour is not silent.
 .
 A regression test (testsuite/proxy-response-line-too-long.test) exercises the
 over-long proxy response line and asserts rsync rejects it cleanly without
 crashing.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/commit/e9b22cd40446bb34d005ecb44519e11795d4be53
Bug: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-53791
