Description: CVE-2026-70454 - rsync-ssl improper TLS certificate validation (CWE-295)
 rsync-ssl (openssl mode 3.2.0-3.2.3, stunnel mode through 3.4.4) failed to
 validate the server certificate against a trusted CA and did not perform
 hostname verification, letting an on-path attacker MITM/decrypt/tamper with
 rsync-over-SSL sessions using an invalid or misissued certificate.
 .
 Backport of the two upstream rsync 3.5.0 fix commits, adapted to the 3.2.3
 tree:
 .
 rsync-ssl: refuse stunnel/gnutls modes without RSYNC_SSL_CA_CERT (unless the
 operator explicitly opts out), bind the server certificate to the requested
 hostname (openssl -verify_hostname, stunnel checkHost) with a
 RSYNC_SSL_SKIP_HOSTNAME_CHECK escape hatch, and reject injection-shaped
 hostnames fail-closed.
 .
 batch.c: single-quote every --write-batch replay-script argument (so shell
 metacharacters stay literal) and refuse a newline in a filter rule written to
 the replay script. The upstream fix also hardened open_batch_files() against
 a planted symlink and a non-regular --read-batch file, but this tree's
 open_batch_files() was already hardened equivalently (open_no_attacker_symlinks()
 plus an S_ISREG check) by the prior CVE-2026-53803 backport, so that part is
 not repeated here.
 .
 io.c: only treat a short read as the EOF sentinel while the fd is still open
 (guard the -2 "peer already gone" sentinel).
 .
 xattrs.c: store the ndx returned by rsync_xal_store() in cache_tmp_xattr(), and
 guard ndx < 0 in set_xattr() before indexing rsync_xal_l.items. The upstream
 guard was written against a 5-arg fd-based set_xattr() from an unrelated
 intervening refactor; it is re-derived here as a simple "if (ndx < 0) return 0;"
 against the target's 4-arg signature.
 .
 A shell-idiom regression test (testsuite/rsync-ssl-cert-validation.test) is
 added in place of the upstream python testsuite cases, which are not shipped in
 this tree.
