+#! /bin/sh
+
+# Regression test for CVE-2026-53783: rrsync must not let a restricted client
+# pivot out of its restricted directory via a symlink / directory-escape,
+# and must fail closed when a path resolves outside the restricted tree.
+#
+# The upstream fix targets the python3 rewrite of rrsync (rsync >= 3.2.4) using
+# a Linux /proc/self/fd inode-pin; Debian 11 ships the legacy perl rrsync, so
+# this test exercises the portable perl backport: realpath-based containment
+# that fails closed, plus disabling the symlink-following options while
+# restricted.
+#
+# NOTE: the perl rrsync intentionally exits non-zero when it rejects a path.
+# runtests.sh runs each test under `sh -e`, so any command substitution that
+# captures a rejecting rrsync would abort the script; every such invocation
+# below is guarded with `|| true`.
+
+. "$suitedir/rsync.fns"
+
+rrsync="$srcdir/support/rrsync"
+
+if test ! -f "$rrsync"; then
+    test_skipped "support/rrsync is not present"
+fi
+
+if perl -e 'use Cwd "abs_path"; use File::Glob ":glob";' 2>/dev/null; then
+    :
+else
+    test_skipped "perl (with Cwd/File::Glob) is required for the perl rrsync"
+fi
+
+# Build a restricted dir and an out-of-tree secret the client must never reach.
+restrict="$scratchdir/restrict"
+outside="$scratchdir/outside"
+makepath "$restrict/sub"
+makepath "$outside"
+echo 'TOP SECRET' >"$outside/secret"
+echo 'in tree'    >"$restrict/ok.txt"
+
+# Install a fake rsync so we can observe exactly which args rrsync would exec
+# without performing a real transfer.  rrsync uses the hard-coded RSYNC path
+# (/usr/bin/rsync); we instead run rrsync under a wrapper by shadowing exec via
+# a small perl driver that sets RSYNC through an env override is not supported,
+# so we assert on rrsync's own accept/reject decision (its exit status and
+# stderr) which is where the security check lives.
+
+run_rrsync() {
+    # $1 = restricted subdir, remaining = the emulated client rsync command.
+    _sub="$1"; shift
+    SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND="$*" \
+	SSH_CONNECTION='127.0.0.1 1 127.0.0.1 22' \
+	perl "$rrsync" "$_sub" 2>"$scratchdir/rrsync.err"
+}
+
+# A helper that expects rrsync to REJECT (non-zero exit) the command.
+expect_reject() {
+    _desc="$1"; shift
+    _sub="$1"; shift
+    if run_rrsync "$_sub" "$@" >/dev/null 2>>"$scratchdir/rrsync.err"; then
+	# rrsync would have exec'd /usr/bin/rsync on failure only if it
+	# accepted; but exec replaces the process. To avoid a real exec we
+	# only reach here when rrsync died before exec (rejection) OR exec
+	# succeeded. Distinguish via the recorded error text below.
+	:
+    fi
+}
+
+# --- Case 1: a plain in-tree pull is accepted (arg validation passes) --------
+# rrsync will attempt to exec /usr/bin/rsync; if that binary is missing the
+# exec fails AFTER validation, which is fine -- we only care that validation
+# did not reject the path.  Capture stderr and assert no "unsafe"/"escape".
+run_rrsync "$restrict" 'rsync --server --sender -vlogDtpr . ok.txt' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
+if grep -qi 'unsafe\|escape\|do not use' "$scratchdir/rrsync.err" 2>/dev/null; then
+    test_fail "Case 1 FAILED: legitimate in-tree path ok.txt was wrongly rejected:
+$(cat "$scratchdir/rrsync.err")"
+fi
+
+# --- Case 2: a symlink that escapes the restricted dir must be rejected ------
+ln -s "$outside" "$restrict/escape"
+run_rrsync "$restrict" 'rsync --server --sender -vlogDtpr . escape/secret' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
+if grep -qi 'unsafe\|escape\|do not use' "$scratchdir/rrsync.err" 2>/dev/null; then
+    :
+else
+    test_fail "Case 2 FAILED: symlink escaping the restricted dir was NOT rejected:
+$(cat "$scratchdir/rrsync.err")"
+fi
+
+# --- Case 3: a symlinked --backup-dir target outside the tree is rejected ----
+run_rrsync "$restrict" 'rsync --server -vlogDtpre.iLsfxC --backup-dir escape/bak . .' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
+if grep -qi 'unsafe\|escape\|do not use\|\.\.' "$scratchdir/rrsync.err" 2>/dev/null; then
+    :
+else
+    test_fail "Case 3 FAILED: --backup-dir escaping via symlink was NOT rejected:
+$(cat "$scratchdir/rrsync.err")"
+fi
+
+# --- Case 4: a bare .. in a path is still rejected (unchanged behaviour) ------
+run_rrsync "$restrict" 'rsync --server --sender -vlogDtpr . ../outside/secret' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
+if grep -qi '\.\.\|unsafe\|escape\|do not use' "$scratchdir/rrsync.err" 2>/dev/null; then
+    :
+else
+    test_fail "Case 4 FAILED: '..' path traversal was NOT rejected:
+$(cat "$scratchdir/rrsync.err")"
+fi
+
+# --- Case 5: while restricted, symlink-following options are disabled ---------
+run_rrsync "$restrict" 'rsync --server --sender -vlogDtprL . ok.txt' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
+if grep -qi 'disabled\|unsafe\|escape' "$scratchdir/rrsync.err" 2>/dev/null; then
+    :
+else
+    test_fail "Case 5 FAILED: symlink-following short option -L was NOT disabled while restricted:
+$(cat "$scratchdir/rrsync.err")"
+fi
+
+echo "all rrsync CVE-2026-53783 checks passed"
+
+# The test harness checks the exit code.
+exit 0
Description: CVE-2026-70462 - reject/cap malicious MSG_IO_TIMEOUT and make set_io_timeout() overflow-safe
 A malicious rsync server can send a MSG_IO_TIMEOUT message to a connected
 client with a non-positive value (e.g. 0), which disabled the client's
 --timeout entirely via set_io_timeout(), allowing the server to hang the
 client indefinitely (denial of service). A very large value (near INT_MAX)
 instead caused a signed integer overflow in the (io_timeout + 1) / 2
 computation in set_io_timeout(), wrapping allowed_lull and select_timeout
 negative and trapping the client in a tight select() loop (EINVAL).
 .
 This backports the upstream fix: read_a_msg() now rejects a non-positive
 MSG_IO_TIMEOUT and caps the accepted value at 24 hours, and set_io_timeout()
 clamps negative input to zero and performs its arithmetic in a wider type
 with a bound on allowed_lull so the derived limits cannot overflow.
