--- /dev/null
+++ b/testsuite/cve-2026-53795-symlink-confine.test
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+#! /bin/sh
+
+# Regression test for CVE-2026-53795 (CWE-59, link following).
+#
+# A local rename / hard-link / basis-open re-resolves a full pathname each time
+# it runs.  If a *parent* component of a destination-tree path is (or is flipped
+# into) a symlink owned by another uid, the operation must NOT follow it out of
+# the destination tree -- doing so lets an attacker redirect a tmp->final rename,
+# a --link-dest hard link, or a --partial-dir basis open to an arbitrary file
+# outside the tree (content injection / arbitrary write / read leak).
+#
+# The upstream fix (rsync 3.5.0, commits a701236c / cfd40f55) confines these
+# sinks with an fd-based ownership walk.  This release already backports the
+# equivalent confinement primitive (secure_relative_open() / do_rename_at() /
+# do_link_at(), from the CVE-2026-29518+ series); this patch closes the two
+# remaining gaps (atomic_create's rename, and one_inplace's fd1 check).
+#
+# The upstream PoCs are cross-uid race harnesses (Python).  This is the
+# deterministic, race-free counterpart: it pre-plants a foreign-owned symlink as
+# a destination parent and asserts rsync writes the received file INSIDE the tree
+# (or fails) rather than following the symlink to the out-of-tree victim.
+# Root-gated, because a cross-uid symlink plant is what makes the flip untrusted;
+# a same-uid symlink is legitimately followed and is not the vulnerability.
+
+. "$suitedir/rsync.fns"
+
+# Need root to plant a symlink owned by a different (untrusted) uid.
+if test x"`id -u 2>/dev/null`" != x"0"; then
+    test_skipped "must be root to plant a foreign-owned symlink for the cross-uid confinement check"
+fi
+
+# Find an untrusted uid to own the planted symlink (prefer nobody).
+att_uid=`id -u nobody 2>/dev/null || true`
+if test x"$att_uid" = x || test x"$att_uid" = x"0"; then
+    att_uid=65534
+fi
+
+cd "$tmpdir" || test_fail "cannot cd to tmpdir"
+
+rm -rf src dest outside
+mkdir src outside
+# The out-of-tree victim that must remain untouched.
+echo "OUTSIDE-VICTIM-UNTOUCHED" > outside/victim
+
+# Source file whose destination path traverses a foreign-owned symlink.
+mkdir src/escape
+echo "LEGIT-TRANSFERRED-CONTENT" > src/escape/victim
+
+# Destination: "escape" is a symlink (owned by an untrusted uid) that points at
+# the out-of-tree directory.  A pre-fix rsync resolves dest/escape/victim
+# through this symlink and clobbers outside/victim.
+mkdir dest
+ln -s "$tmpdir/outside" dest/escape
+chown -h "$att_uid" dest/escape 2>/dev/null \
+    || test_skipped "cannot chown the planted symlink to an untrusted uid"
+
+# Sanity: the plant really is a foreign-owned symlink.
+is_a_link dest/escape || test_fail "setup: dest/escape is not a symlink"
+
+# Run the transfer.  With an absolute --temp-dir the tmp->final rename is a full
+# path re-resolution (the classic injection vector); confinement must apply.
+mkdir tmp
+$RSYNC -r --temp-dir="$tmpdir/tmp" src/escape/ dest/escape/ >rsync.out 2>&1 || true
+
+# The out-of-tree victim must be byte-for-byte unchanged.
+if test "`cat outside/victim`" != "OUTSIDE-VICTIM-UNTOUCHED"; then
+    cat rsync.out >&2
+    test_fail "CVE-2026-53795: rsync followed a foreign-owned parent symlink and wrote OUTSIDE the destination tree"
+fi
+
+echo "CVE-2026-53795 confinement OK: out-of-tree victim untouched"
+exit 0
Description: CVE-2026-70453 - bound the hash_search() chain walk (issue #217)
 A weak (rolling) checksum that collides on a very long hash-table chain --
 common in disk/VM images, which contain large runs of identical blocks --
 made hash_search()'s inner loop walk the entire chain for every file offset.
 This is an O(file_size * chain_length) scan that pegs a CPU at 100% for hours
 with no visible progress (a denial of service against the sender).
 .
 This backport caps the number of same-weak-checksum candidates examined per
 offset at MAX_CHAIN_LEN (1024) in match.c. When the cap is reached the offset
 is treated as a non-match and the data is sent literally, so the transfer
 stays byte-for-byte correct. The change is sender-side only: it alters no
 checksum, emitted byte, or protocol field, so a capped sender interoperates
 with any receiver.
 .
 A shell regression test (testsuite/hashsearch-chain.test) is added in place of
 the upstream Python test (testsuite/hashsearch-chain_test.py), which targets a
 newer testsuite harness not present in this rsync version. It builds a
 pathological long-chain basis and asserts, via the --debug=deltasum1
 false_alarms/hash_hits ratio, that the per-offset chain walk stays bounded,
 while verifying the transferred file is identical to the source.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/commit/e6956e0a30cb360b413ce2ad8df89204a1951059
Bug: https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/issues/217
Author: Wayne Davison <wayne@opencoder.net>
Last-Update: 2026-08-14
