Description: CVE-2026-70456 - fix off-by-one heap write in read_args() (io.c)
 After the '.' separator, read_args() delegates argv growth to glob_expand(),
 which reserves room (glob.argc + 1) for the entry it adds but not for the
 trailing NULL that read_args() writes after the loop. When a single post-dot
 glob lands argc exactly on maxargs, no growth happens and the final
 "argv[argc] = NULL" is an 8-byte NULL write one slot past the argv
 allocation. This is reachable in the per-connection daemon child from a
 malicious peer. Reserve the +1 slot before the NULL store.
 .
 Backport of the io.c read_args() slice only from upstream commit
 3eeedf8d08178ff2f354c09be64eb0f56fa8454f (released in rsync 3.5.0); the
 unrelated hardening hunks in that commit are out of scope for this CVE.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/commit/3eeedf8d08178ff2f354c09be64eb0f56fa8454f
Author: Wayne Davison <wayne@opencoder.net>
Last-Update: 2026-08-14
---
--- a/io.c
+++ b/io.c
@@ -1285,6 +1285,13 @@ void read_args(int f_in, char *mod_name,
 				dot_pos = argc;
 		}
 	}
+	/* glob_expand()/glob_match() reserve glob.argc+1 slots -- room for the
+	 * entry being added but not for this trailing NULL.  A post-dot line
+	 * whose glob splits land argc on exactly maxargs (or any later
+	 * ENSURE_MEMSPACE doubling boundary) would otherwise make the next
+	 * store an 8-byte NULL write one slot past the argv allocation. */
+	if (argc >= maxargs)
+		argv = realloc_array(argv, char *, maxargs = argc + 1);
 	argv[argc] = NULL;

 	glob_expand(NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
Description: Fix CVE-2026-70460 — confine operator-supplied dirs to the served module
 rsync 3.2.3 only confines operator-/peer-supplied directory paths
 (--temp-dir, --backup-dir, --partial-dir and the --link-dest/--compare-dest/
 --copy-dest alt-dest basis) to a served daemon module LEXICALLY, via
 sanitize_path() (leading-slash clamp + ".." stripping) and the daemon filter
 list.  Neither performs any PHYSICAL path resolution, so a symlink component
 that already exists inside the module tree (a link the ownership/filter rules
 already trust) is followed by the subsequent libc-resolved symlink()/open()/
 mknod()/rename() call and the write lands outside the module root — a
 link-following / path-confinement bypass (CWE-59) yielding an arbitrary file
 write relative to the module root's parent.
 .
 The upstream fix (rsync 3.5.0, commits defd7110 and 71b01c4) relies on a
 secure-path-resolution subsystem (operator_path_resolve / owner_walk_parent /
 confine_operator_path) that does not exist in 3.2.3, so it cannot be applied
 literally.  This is a target-native reimplementation of the same protection
 using a new realpath()-based confinement helper, path_is_module_confined(),
 wired into the two existing sanitize_paths sites (parse_arguments() for the
 temp/backup dirs, server_recv() for the alt-dest basis and partial dir).  The
 helper is a no-op unless sanitize_paths is set (a confined daemon module), so
 local, non-daemon transfers keep their legacy behavior.  For a confined module
 the daemon has already change_dir()'d into the module root, so realpath(".")
 is the confinement anchor; the operator path's deepest existing ancestor is
 canonicalized and must equal the module root or lie strictly beneath it, else
 the options are rejected via the existing options_rejected handler.
