+connect deny-match && test_fail "control: [deny-match] (hosts deny = 127.0.0.0/8) should be DENIED but succeeded"
+connect deny-nomatch || test_fail "control: [deny-nomatch] (hosts deny = 10.0.0.0/8, non-matching) should be ALLOWED but was refused"
+
+# The fix: an unresolvable deny-list hostname token must fail CLOSED.
+connect deny-unresolvable && test_fail "[deny-unresolvable] (hosts deny = nope.nonexistent.invalid) was ALLOWED: an unresolvable deny token failed OPEN (CVE-2026-70452)"
+
+echo "daemon-deny-dns-failopen: unresolvable hosts-deny token fails closed"
+exit 0
Description: Fix CVE-2026-53790 — command/argument injection via unsanitized hostnames
 rsync 3.2.3 interpolates untrusted hostnames/hostspecs into shell command
 templates and process argv without validation or quoting, allowing OS command
 injection and argument (leading-dash / CRLF) injection. This backports the
 self-contained hardening primitives from the upstream v3.5.0 release
 (GHSA-5hcf-7xxm-rmqq):
 .
   * socket.c: validate the host against a safe character set and single-quote
     it before substituting it into the RSYNC_CONNECT_PROG %H token that is run
     via system()/sh -c (the primary command-injection sink).
   * socket.c: reject ASCII control bytes in the HTTP proxy CONNECT host to
     prevent CRLF request/header smuggling.
   * main.c: refuse a remote-shell hostname that begins with '-' so it cannot be
     smuggled to ssh/rsh as an option (argument injection).
 .
